method-pyramid
    Pyramid
method-depth
    Depth
method-wavelet
    Wavelet
method-pyramid-description
    Blends the sharpest detail of every frame at each scale. The all-around choice; an edge in front of a far background can get a soft glow.
method-depth-description
    Finds the sharpest frame at each spot, then blends neighbors. Smooth on plain backgrounds and clean beside bright edges; hairs and fine texture come out softer than Pyramid.
method-wavelet-description
    Takes each pixel's color whole from one frame. For fine crossing detail; a plain background can come out grainy. Denoise keeps noise in plain areas from being picked up as detail.
method-pyramid-summary
    Pyramid · S<smoothness>
method-depth-summary
    Depth · R<radius> S<smoothing> B<blend>
method-wavelet-summary
    Wavelet · D<denoise>
method-wavelet-consistent-summary
    Wavelet · D<denoise> C<consistency>
align-ecc
    ECC (affine)
align-grid
    Grid search (similarity)
setting-smoothness-help
    Higher shares each spot among more frames, so noise averages out and fine detail softens.
setting-smoothing-help
    Higher evens out plain areas where no frame is clearly the sharpest; lower keeps the outline of a subject against a far background narrow.
setting-denoise-help
    Higher removes more noise, and more of the faintest texture with it.
setting-radius-help
    Higher decides each spot's frame over a larger area, which is steadier but can soften thin hairs.
setting-blend-range-help
    Lower takes most spots from a single frame; higher mixes neighboring frames more, which can look softer.
setting-aligner-help
    ECC lines up small shifts, turns and changes in size closely; Grid search finds frames that moved further between shots.
setting-crop-help
    On keeps only the area every frame covers; off keeps the whole frame, with softer edges where some frames don't reach.
setting-decoder-help
    Changes which decoder reads this stack's camera RAW frames; the two crop and color them differently.
order-name
    Name
order-capture-time
    Capture time
order-missing-times (count=0)
    0 frames have no capture time, so the frames stay in name order
